---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import SiteHeader from "../../components/SiteHeader.astro";
import SiteFooter from "../../components/SiteFooter.astro";
import "../../styles/global.css";
---
<Layout
  title="How to Wash Hanfu: Fabric Care Guide"
  description="How to wash Hanfu safely: hand-wash silk, machine-wash blends, remove stains, dry flat, iron, and store. Fabric-by-fabric care instructions for cotton, ramie, and silk Hanfu."
  canonicalUrl="https://dresses-hanfu.com/guides/washing"
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: "Guides", url: "/guides" }, { name: "How to Wash Hanfu", url: "/guides/washing" }]}
  articleSchema={{
    datePublished: "2026-08-01",
    dateModified: "2026-08-08",
    author: { "@type": "Person", "name": "Hanfu Dress Guide Editorial Team" }
  }}
>
  <SiteHeader active="/guides/how-to-wear" />
  <main>
    <article>
      <section class="section" style="padding-bottom: 2rem;">
        <div class="container-narrow">
          <p style="font-size:0.8125rem;color:var(--color-muted);margin-bottom:1rem;"><a href="/" style="color:var(--color-accent);">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/guides" style="color:var(--color-accent);">Guides</a> &rsaquo; How to Wash Hanfu</p>
          <h1>How to Wash Hanfu: Care by Fabric</h1>
          <p class="lead" style="font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--color-muted);margin-top:1.5rem;line-height:1.55;">
            The number one way people ruin Hanfu is washing it like regular clothing. Silk and embroidered pieces need different care than cotton and blends. This guide covers hand-washing silk, machine-safe blends, stain removal, drying, ironing, and storage — step by step.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section" style="padding-top:0;">
        <div class="container-narrow">
          <h2>First: Read the Care Label</h2>
          <p class="text-muted">
            Every repTable manufacturer should tag material composition and washing instructions. If a piece is 100% silk, it is almost always hand-wash or dry-clean only. If it's a polyester blend, you can usually use a gentle machine cycle. When in doubt, treat the garment like silk.
          </p>

          <h2 style="margin-top:3rem;">Washing Silk Hanfu (Hand-Wash)</h2>
          <div class="step-list" style="max-width:100%;">
            <div class="step-row">
              <span class="step-number">1</span>
              <div class="step-content">
                <h4>Fill a Basin with Cool Water</h4>
                <p>Cold water only — heat shrinks silk and sets stains. Use a basin, not the sink drain where the garment can clog. Add a few drops of gentle silk detergent or baby shampoo.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="step-row">
              <span class="step-number">2</span>
              <div class="step-content">
                <h4>Submerge and Swirl, Don't Scrub</h4>
                <p>Press the garment up and down in the water rather than twisting or rubbing. Let it soak 3-5 minutes, then drain and rinse with clear cool water.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="step-row">
              <span class="step-number">3</span>
              <div class="step-content">
                <h4>Press Out Water, Don't Wring</h4>
                <p>Roll the garment in a clean, dry towel and press to absorb water. Never wring or twist silk — the fibers break.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="step-row">
              <span class="step-number">4</span>
              <div class="step-content">
                <h4>Dry Flat in the Shade</h4>
                <p>Lay flat on a dry towel out of direct sunlight. Do not hang a heavy silk robe — the weight can stretch it. Full drying can take 1-2 days.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="step-row">
              <span class="step-number">5</span>
              <div class="step-content">
                <h4>Iron on the Coolest Setting</h4>
                <p>Iron while slightly damp, inside-out, on the coolest setting, with a pressing cloth between the iron and the silk. Steam lightly or skip steaming on fragile embroidery.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h2 style="margin-top:3rem;">Washing Cotton, Ramie, and Blends</h2>
          <p class="text-muted">
            Cotton and blends are far more forgiving:
          </p>
          <ul style="list-style:disc;padding-left:1.5rem;color:var(--color-muted);font-size:0.9375rem;line-height:1.8;">
            <li>Machine-wash on a <strong>gentle cycle, cold water</strong>, inside out, ideally in a mesh garment bag.</li>
            <li>Use a <strong>gentle detergent</strong>; avoid bleach on dark colors and embroidery.</li>
            <li>Hand-wash ramie to preserve its crisp texture, or gentle-machine; ramie creases easily — dry flat or hang to minimize wrinkles.</li>
            <li>Tumble dry on low, or better, <strong>air dry flat</strong>.</li>
          </ul>

          <h2 style="margin-top:3rem;">Stain Removal</h2>
          <ul style="list-style:disc;padding-left:1.5rem;color:var(--color-muted);font-size:0.9375rem;line-height:1.8;">
            <li><strong>Act fast</strong> — the longer a stain sits, the harder it is to remove.</li>
            <li><strong>Oil/food:</strong> blot with a clean white cloth, gently work in a drop of dish soap, rinse carefully (test on an hidden area first for silk).</li>
            <li><strong>Makeup:</strong> use a gentle makeup-remover; test on the seam first.</li>
            <li><strong>Never rub silk</strong> with a brush — use a fingertip or a soft cloth.</li>
            <li>For stubborn stains on silk, take the piece to a dry cleaner that handles silk.</li>
          </ul>

          <h2 style="margin-top:3rem;">Storage</h2>
          <ul style="list-style:disc;padding-left:1.5rem;color:var(--color-muted);font-size:0.9375rem;line-height:1.8;">
            <li><strong>Fold, don't hang</strong> heavy quilting or embroidered pieces; hanging pulls the weave.</li>
            <li>Store in a <strong>cool, dry, dark place</strong> — silk can yellow in direct sun.</li>
            <li>Use <strong>acid-free tissue</strong> between layers of embroidery to protect needlework.</li>
            <li><strong>Avoid mothballs</strong> near silk; use cedar or lavender sachets instead.</li>
            <li>Dehumidify if storage is damp; silk absorbs moisture/humidity.</li>
          </ul>

          <h2 style="margin-top:3rem;">Quick Care Reference Table</h2>
          <div style="overflow-x:auto;">
            <table class="size-ref-table" style="margin-top:1rem;">
              <thead><tr><th>Fabric</th><th>Wash</th><th>Dry</th><th>Iron</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td><strong>Pure silk</strong></td><td>Hand-wash cold, gentle soap</td><td>Flat, shade, roll-towel</td><td>Low, inside, press cloth</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Rayon</strong></td><td>Hand-wash cold or dry clean</td><td>Flat, shade</td><td>Low, through cloth</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Cotton</strong></td><td>Machine, cold, gentle, mesh bag</td><td>Air dry / low tumble</td><td>Medium</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Ramie/hemp</strong></td><td>Machine cold or hand</td><td>Air dry flat</td><td>Medium-high while damp</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Polyester blend</strong></td><td>Machine, gentle, cold</td><td>Low tumble or hang</td><td>Low</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <div style="margin-top:3rem;padding:2rem;background:var(--color-accent-light);border-radius:var(--radius-md);">
            <p style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:500;font-family:var(--font-display);">Continue Learning</p>
            <div style="display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:0.75rem;margin-top:1rem;">
              <a href="/guides/fabrics" class="btn btn-outline">Hanfu Fabrics</a>
              <a href="/guides/sizing" class="btn btn-outline">Size Guide</a>
              <a href="/guides/how-to-wear" class="btn btn-outline">How to Wear</a>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
    </article>
  </main>
  <SiteFooter />
</Layout>